  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nEXTREME HEAT...The main axis of the heat ridge will remain over \nthe Rockies through the weekend into early next week. However, \nsurface fronts will maintain the extensive warm sector across \nthe WFO MPX coverage area, including elevated low-mid level \nmoisture advection, through the weekend. This means not just the\ndaytime hours with highs in the low-mid 90s today and into the \nmid-upper 90s Sunday but also overnight lows mainly in the low- \nmid 70s tonight and Sunday night. With dewpoints into the upper \n60s to mid 70s (even upper 70s tomorrow), this will prolong \ndangerous heat conditions through Sunday night, hence the \nexpansion of the Extreme Heat Warning from much of our MN area \nto the remainder of our area tonight into tomorrow. Sunday will \nbe by far the hottest day with HI's for nearly our entire area \nreaching 105-110 and WBGT's climbing into the upper 80s for the \nvast majority of our area. The heat ridge will retreat westward \nfor early next week while a weak frontal boundary drops south, \nwhich will allow dewpoints to drop off appreciably, back to the \n60s to near 70 for the rest of the week while gradually fall \nback to the upper 80s to lower 90s. So, not to say it won't at \nall be warm/humid depending on location within our 51 counties, \nbut it will not be nearly as hot/muggy as today through Sunday.\n\nTHUNDERSTORM POTENTIAL...Quite the tricky and low confidence\nforecast when it comes to the where/when of any convection which \nmay develop over the WFO MPX coverage area. Quiet thus far \ntoday, not only over our area but also upstream over the eastern\nDakotas. Starting to see some development over western SD in \nresponse to strong surface heating and modest to steep lapse \nrates and inherent instability. 12z RAOBs and model soundings \nstill show plenty of mid-level capping with warm H85-H7 \ntemperatures that seem to be diminished in pockets, similar to \nwhere there are spots of stronger surface heating than others. \nThe idea is that storms will blossom over eastern SD and drift \nESE into southwestern MN later this afternoon through this \nevening. However, only a few CAMs indicate such idea, and even \nfewer run-to-run today. Some CAMs indicate no convection at all.\nSo this is a highly conditional setup for today due to the \nstrong low-mid level capping (indicative per the continual low \nlevel stratus today) and the patchwork of integrating higher \ninstability, plus with fairly unimpressive dynamics aloft. \n\nOnce past tonight's potential convection (and there is even the \nidea among a few CAMs of a sunrise-ish complex over eastern MN \ninto western WI, so still plenty of uncertainty), then there \nlooks to be a better organization Sunday afternoon-evening \ndespite continued wide-ranging model solutions. More NW flow \naloft may allow a few disturbances to rush through the area, \nespecially within the higher temp/dewpoint regime leading to \nsteep lapse rates and poorer capping with a weak frontal \nboundary dropping south. Still rather disorganized in nature, \nthere may be a few clusters of storms late day into the evening \nwith the main \"target\" area as far western into far southern MN.\n\n\nMONDAY ONWARD...A more tranquil pattern looks to settle in\nstarting on Monday with drier air and a slow drop of\ntemperatures as the week progresses. Overall, highs will return \nto the mid 80s to lower 90s as a general range throughout the \nweek while dewpoints fall back to the 60s to near 70 during the \ndaytime hours. It is possible that the TC metro may need a Heat \nAdvisory on Monday but otherwise the forecast does not seem to \nsupport any additional heat headlines beyond the weekend. More \npronounced northwest flow aloft will prevail throughout the week\nwith the occasional disturbance drifting through the Upper \nMidwest. No appreciable surface boundaries are expected for our \nregion, which will limit any chances for precipitation due to \nthe lack of forcing and lack of low level convergence features. \nThe best chance after this weekend looks to come late Thursday \nthrough early Friday, mainly owing to a fairly well-defined \nshortwave trough aloft expected to move through the region. \nNothing impressive nor any surface features, but a swath of \ndeeper moisture accompanying the trough looks to produce \nscattered showers/thunderstorms for late Thu into Fri.",
